course: dictionary
lesson: 3748
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word fluff can mean soft bits of wool or dust, the soft fur of a baby animal, or talk with no real meaning. To find the right meaning, read the words around it. «A frightened cat fluffs out its fur to look bigger» uses the word as a verb, so it must mean to make something puff up.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably found it. «Tiny birds have fluffy down to keep warm» is about the soft feathers of young birds, not about dust. Ask yourself: is this a thing, or an action? That tells you if fluff is a noun or a verb.
explain: Some meanings are more about feelings or judgement. When a teacher says «cut the fluff» in a writing lesson, she means remove words that add nothing. «Ben fluffed his lines in the play» means he did it badly and made a mistake. The context shows you which meaning the writer wants.
fact: Some words have several meanings, and the context tells you which one is being used.
fact: As a noun, «fluff» means small soft bits of wool, dust, hair or feathers, or the soft fur of a young animal.
fact: As a verb, «fluff up» means to shake or pat something so it becomes softer and fuller, and «fluff» can mean to do something badly.
fact: «Fluff» can also mean talk or writing with little real meaning, which is why teachers say «cut the fluff».
fact: Tumble dryers collect fluff in a filter, and the filter must be cleaned because fluff can catch fire.
exercise: «The tumble dryer filter was full of grey fluff.» What is fluff here? | Money left in your pocket | *Small soft bits of wool, dust or hair | A kind of cake | A loud noise from the machine
exercise: «A frightened cat fluffs out its fur to look bigger.» What does fluffs out mean here? | *Makes its fur puff up and stand out | Washes its fur in the rain | Licks its fur flat and smooth | Cuts its fur short
exercise: Your teacher says «cut the fluff» about your story. What should you do? | Add more soft and fluffy words | Read it out loud very slowly | Make the story longer with a fluffy cat | *Remove the words that add nothing
test: «Her baby chick was covered in soft yellow fluff.» Which meaning fits? | *The soft feathers of a young bird | A mistake in a play | Talk with no real meaning | Dust under the bed
test: «Ben fluffed his lines in the school play.» What does fluffed mean here? | Sang them very loudly | Shook them so they became soft | *Did them badly and made a mistake | Practised them every day
exam: «A good owner watches for a fluffed-up, quiet bird, because it may be ill.» What does fluffed-up mean here? | Its feathers have been cut short | Its wings are flapping very fast | *Its feathers are puffed out, and it looks round and sleepy | It is singing loudly
